  "worked_solution": "First, we take the factor $7$ outside the summation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}7\u0394{z}_{k}=7{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}\u0394{z}_{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}\u0394{z}_{k}$ is just the length of the interval $[4,9],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}\u0394{z}_{k} & =({z}_{1}-{z}_{0})+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})+({z}_{3}-{z}_{2})+\u22ef+({z}_{q}-{z}_{q-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{z}_{1}}-{z}_{0})+(\\cancel{{z}_{2}}-\\cancel{{z}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{z}_{3}}-\\cancel{{z}_{2}})+\u22ef+({z}_{q}-\\cancel{{z}_{q-1}}) \\\\  & =-{z}_{0}+{z}_{q} \\\\  & ={z}_{q}-{z}_{0} \\\\  & =9-4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}\u0394{z}_{k}=7\\cdot 5=35.\n$$",
